#import "main-defs.typ": *
#import "statements.typ": *

== Finite Groups with $B N$-Pairs <sec:finite-groups-bn-pairs>

=== The Bruhat Decomposition <ss:bruhat-decomposition>

We begin with an axiomatic approach to finite groups of Lie type due to J. Tits
@bib:Tits1962. The axioms capture the essential features of Chevalley's analysis
in @bib:Chevalley1955 of the structure of what are now called Chevalley groups.
They are also part of the foundations of the theory of buildings, and lead to a
geometrical classification of the finite Chevalley groups (Tits @bib:Tits1974).
In this subsection we give the axioms for $B N$-pairs, and we derive some of
their consequences. These include a double coset decomposition of a group with a
$B N$-pair (called the Bruhat decomposition); its role in the study of groups of
Lie type is analogous to that of the root space decomposition of a semisimple
Lie algebra over $CC$ relative to a Cartan subalgebra. (For the construction of
Chevalley groups from semisimple Lie algebras, see Chevalley @bib:Chevalley1955,
Steinberg @bib:Steinberg1967, or Carter @bib:Carter1972b.)

#definitions[
  A _finite group with a $B N$-pair_#footnote[
    Also called a _Tits system_; see Bourbaki @bib:Bourbaki1968.
  ] <fn:bn-pair-tits-system>
  is a finite group $G$ containing a pair of subgroups $B,N$ satisfying the
  following axioms:

  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [#case[$G=chevron.l B,N chevron.r$.]
      <case:bn-pair-generation>],
    [#case[$B inter N ⊴ N$.]
      <case:bn-pair-normal-intersection>],
    [#case[
      Let $W=N/(B inter N)$, and for each $w in W$ choose a coset representative
      $dot(w) in N$. Then $W$ is generated by a set $S={s_1,...,s_n}$ of
      involutions such that
      $
        dot(s)_i B dot(w) subset.eq B dot(w) B union B dot(s)_i dot(w) B
      $ <eq:bn-pair-double-coset-axiom>
      and
      $ dot(s)_i B dot(s)_i != B $
      <eq:bn-pair-nondegeneracy-axiom>
      for each $w in W$ and each $s_i in S$.
    ] <case:bn-pair-distinguished-involutions>],
  )
] <def:finite-group-bn-pair>
#idx("BN-pair", target: [@def:finite-group-bn-pair])

#source(593)
For a group $G$ satisfying the axioms, we will usually denote $B inter N$ by
$T$, and call the group $W=N/T$ the _Weyl group_ associated with the $B N$-pair.
The generators $S$ of $W$ will be called the _distinguished generators_ of $W$,
and the cardinality $|S|$ is called the _rank_ of the $B N$-pair. (We shall see
later that both $S$ and the rank are uniquely determined.) The subgroup $B$ is
called a #idx("Borel subgroup")_Borel subgroup_ of $G$.

We proceed to derive consequences of the axioms. It may be useful for a reader
who is unfamiliar with these things to read §~@ss:bruhat-decomposition in
parallel with §~@ss:bn-pair-examples, where an important family of examples of
$B N$-pairs is constructed.

Our first remark concerning a group with a $B N$-pair is that, since
$T subset.eq B$, subsets of $G$ of the form $dot(w) B$, $B dot(w) B$,
$attach(B, tl: dot(w))$, etc. are independent of the choice of the coset
representative $dot(w)$, and can thus be written unambiguously as
$ w B, B w B, attach(B, tl: w), quad "for" w in W. $
We also note that since $W$ has a set of involutory generators $S$, we can
define the _length_ $l(w)$ of elements $w in W$ as in
@def:involutory-generator-length-exchange, as well as the concept of _reduced
expression_ for elements of $W$. We now have:

#theorem(title: [Bruhat Decomposition])[
  Let $G$ be a finite group with a $B N$-pair. Then
  $ G=union.dot.big_(w in W) B w B. $
  In particular, the map $w arrow.r B w B$ gives a bijection:
  $W arrow.l.r B backslash G slash B$.
] <th:bruhat-decomposition>
#idx("Bruhat decomposition", target: [@th:bruhat-decomposition])

#proof[
  Let $X=union.big_w B w B$, a subset of $G$ containing both $B$ and $N$. To
  prove the first statement in the theorem, we need only show that $X$ is a
  subgroup of $G$. For this, it suffices to check that $b X=X$ for all $b in B$
  (which is obvious), and that#footnote[
    For $z in W$, $z X$ means $dot(z) X$, where $dot(z) in N$ is a coset
    representative of $z$. Since $X$ is a union of double cosets of the form
    $B w B$, our earlier remarks show that $z X$ depends only on $z$, and not on
    the choice of the coset representative $dot(z)$.
  ] <fn:weyl-action-double-coset-union>
  $z X=X$ for all $z in W$. Since the elements of $S$ generate $W$, we need only
  verify that $s_i X=X$ for each $s_i in S$. However, this is an immediate
  consequence of @eq:bn-pair-double-coset-axiom.

  For the second part of the theorem, we must show that if $w!=w'$ in $W$, then
  $B w B!=B w' B$. Let $m=l(w')$, the length of $w'$ as a product of involutions
  in $S$, as in @def:involutory-generator-length-exchange. We use induction on
  $m$, noting that when $m=0$ we have $w'=1$; clearly $B w B!=B$, since
  otherwise $dot(w) in B inter N$, so $w=1$. We may thus assume that $w!=w'$,
  and that
  $ l(w)>=l(w')=m>0. $
  Then for some $s_i in S$ we have $l(s_i w')<=m-1$, so $l(w)!=l(s_i w')$, and
  therefore $w!=s_i w'$. We also have $s_i w!=s_i w'$, so by the induction
  hypothesis, both $B s_i w B$ and #source(594) $B w B$ are distinct from
  $B s_i w' B$. By @eq:bn-pair-double-coset-axiom this implies that
  $ (B s_i B)(B w B) inter B s_i w' B=emptyset. $
  On the other hand, $B s_i w' B subset.eq (B s_i B)(B w' B)$, so it follows
  that $B w B!=B w' B$, as required.
]

Throughout the rest of §~@ss:bruhat-decomposition, we assume that $G$ is a group
with a $B N$-pair, with Weyl group $W$ and set of distinguished generators $S$
of $W$. The next two results describe, to some extent, the multiplication of
double cosets.

#proposition[
  If $w in W$ and $s_i in S$ are such that $l(s_i w)>=l(w)$, then
  $s_i B w subset.eq B s_i w B$.
] <prop:bruhat-length-increasing-product>

#proof[
  We use induction on $l(w)$, noting that the result is clear if $l(w)=0$. We
  may therefore assume $l(w)>0$, and find $s_j in S$ such that $w=w' s_j$, with
  $l(w')<=l(w)-1$. Suppose the result is false in this situation; then by
  @eq:bn-pair-double-coset-axiom we have $s_i B w inter B w B!=emptyset$, so
  $s_i B w' inter B w B s_j!=emptyset$. Since $l(s_i w)>=l(w)$ and
  $s_i w=s_i w' s_j$, we also have $l(s_i w')>=l(w')$, since otherwise
  $l(s_i w)<=l(s_i w')+1<l(w')+1<=l(w)$. Then, by the induction hypothesis,
  $s_i B w' subset.eq B s_i w' B$, so
  $ B s_i w' B inter B w B s_j!=emptyset $
  by the previous discussion. Now
  $ w B s_j subset.eq B w s_j B union B w B $
  by @eq:bn-pair-double-coset-axiom, so $B s_i w' B$ is either $B w s_j B$ or
  $B w B$. Then $s_i w'=w s_j$ or $s_i w'=w$ by @th:bruhat-decomposition. These
  are both easily seen to be impossible, and the result follows. Note that the
  first equality implies $s_i=1$, which is ruled out by
  @eq:bn-pair-nondegeneracy-axiom; this is the first use of that axiom.
]

We now have the complementary statement:

#proposition[
  If $l(s_i w)<=l(w)$, where $s_i in S$ and $w in W$, then
  $s_i B w inter B w B!=emptyset$.
] <prop:bruhat-length-decreasing-intersection>

#proof[
  By @eq:bn-pair-double-coset-axiom we have
  $s_i B s_i subset.eq B union B s_i B$, and $s_i B s_i inter B s_i B!=emptyset$
  by @eq:bn-pair-nondegeneracy-axiom. Then $s_i B inter B s_i B s_i!=emptyset$,
  and hence
  $ s_i B w inter B s_i B s_i w!=emptyset. $
  Since $l(s_i s_i w)=l(w)>=l(s_i w)$, we have
  $ B s_i B s_i w subset.eq B w B $
  by @prop:bruhat-length-increasing-product; hence
  $s_i B w inter B w B!=emptyset$, as required.
]

#source(595)
#corollary[
  Let $w in W$ and $s_i in S$. Then
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [#case[$l(s_i w)!=l(w)$.] <case:bn-length-no-equality>],
    [#case[$l(s_i w)=l(w) plus.minus 1$.] <case:bn-length-unit-change>],
    [#case[$l(s_i w)<l(w) arrow.r s_i B subset.eq B w B w^(-1) B$.]
      <case:bn-descent-conjugate-borel>],
  )
] <cor:bn-pair-length-properties>

The proofs of parts~@case:bn-length-no-equality and @case:bn-length-unit-change
are left as exercises, using @prop:bruhat-length-increasing-product and
@prop:bruhat-length-decreasing-intersection. For
part~@case:bn-descent-conjugate-borel, we have $s_i B w inter B w B!=emptyset$,
and the result follows.

These apparently innocuous results are surprisingly powerful, as is shown by the
next result, which will imply that the Weyl group of a finite group with a
$B N$-pair is always a Coxeter group.

#proposition[
  The set of distinguished generators $S$ of $W$ satisfies the Exchange
  Condition (see @th:coxeter-exchange-condition), namely: if the elements
  $s_(i(0)),...,s_(i(m)) in S$ are such that
  $
    l(s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(m)))=m quad "and"
    l(s_(i(0))s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(m)))<m+1,
  $
  then for some $k$, $1<=k<=m$, we have
  $ s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(k))=s_(i(0)) dots s_(i(k-1)). $
] <prop:bn-pair-exchange-condition>
#idx("Exchange condition", target: [@prop:bn-pair-exchange-condition])

#proof[
  Let $w in W$, and let $w'=s_(j(1)) dots s_(j(p))$ be a product of involutions
  from $S$. By repeated use of @eq:bn-pair-double-coset-axiom, we obtain
  $ w B w' subset.eq union.big B w s_(j(q_1)) dots s_(j(q_t)) B, $
  where the union is taken over all subsequences of $[1,p]$,
  $ 1<=q_1<q_2<dots<q_t<=p. $
  Now let $w=s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(m))$, so $l(w)=m$ and $l(s_(i(0))w)<m+1$ by
  hypothesis. Then by
  @cor:bn-pair-length-properties@case:bn-descent-conjugate-borel and the
  preceding remark, we have
  $
    s_(i(0)) B subset.eq B w B w^(-1) B
    subset.eq union.big B w s_(i(p_1)) dots s_(i(p_k)) B,
  $
  where
  $ m>=p_1>p_2>dots>p_k>=1. $
  This implies, by @th:bruhat-decomposition, that
  $ s_(i(0))=w s_(i(p_1)) dots s_(i(p_k)) $
  for some subsequence ${p_i}$ as above. Since $l(s_(i(0)))=1$ and
  $l(w s_j)=l(w) plus.minus 1$ by #source(596)
  @cor:bn-pair-length-properties@case:bn-length-unit-change, then necessarily
  $k=m-1$, and so
  $ s_(i(0))=w s_(i(m)) dots hat(s)_(i(j)) dots s_(i(1)) $
  with one factor omitted. This is the Exchange Condition.
]

As an immediate application we have the important result:

#theorem[
  Let $W$ be the Weyl group of a finite group with a $B N$-pair, and let $S$ be
  the set of distinguished generators of $W$, as in @def:finite-group-bn-pair.
  Then $(W,S)$ is a Coxeter system (see @def:finite-coxeter-system).
] <th:bn-pair-weyl-coxeter-system>
#idx("Coxeter system", target: [@th:bn-pair-weyl-coxeter-system])

The proof is a direct application of @prop:bn-pair-exchange-condition and the
proof of @th:finite-reflection-group-coxeter-presentation.

=== Examples of $B N$-Pairs <ss:bn-pair-examples>

With some exceptions in low ranks, finite groups with $B N$-pairs tend to occur
in infinite families ${G(bold(F)_q)}$, parametrized by finite fields
$bold(F)_q$, with all the groups in the family having the same Weyl group. In
this subsection, we shall construct such a family, namely the #idx(
  "General linear group",
)general linear groups ${GL_(n+1) lr((bold(F)_q))}$ for a fixed positive integer
$n$, consisting of all invertible $(n+1) times (n+1)$-matrices with entries in a
finite field $bold(F)_q$.

In this case, it is no more difficult to consider the groups
$ G=GL_(n+1) lr((K)), quad n>=1, $
where $K$ is an arbitrary field (of course, $G$ may now be infinite). We define
subgroups of $G$ as follows:
$
  B & = "group of upper triangular matrices"
      ={(a_(i j)) in G:a_(i j)=0 quad "if" i>j}, \
  N & = "group of monomial matrices in" G, \
  T & = "group of diagonal matrices in" G,
$
where a monomial matrix is an element $g in G$ such that each row and each
column of $g$ contains exactly one nonzero entry. Clearly $T=B inter N$. We now
prove, following Bourbaki @bib:Bourbaki1968\:

#theorem[
  The subgroups $B$ and $N$ defined above form a $B N$-pair in
  $G=GL_(n+1) lr((K))$ of rank $n$, whose Weyl group $W$ is isomorphic to the
  symmetric group $S_(n+1)$.
] <th:general-linear-bn-pair>
#idx("BN-pair", target: [@th:general-linear-bn-pair])

#proof[
  Let ${e_1,...,e_(n+1)}$ be a basis for the vector space $K^(n+1)$ on which $G$
  acts. From elementary linear algebra, we know that $G$ is generated by the
  diagonal matrices together with the elementary matrices
  $
    bold(E)_(i j) lr((c))=I+c bold(e)_(i j),
    quad i!=j, quad c in K,
  $
  #source(597) where $bold(e)_(i j)$ is a matrix with 1 in the $(i,j)$ position
  and zeros elsewhere. Another elementary fact is that rows or columns of a
  matrix $g in G$ can be interchanged by left or right multiplication by
  permutation matrices; these belong to the subgroup $N$. The subgroup $B$
  contains all matrices ${bold(E)_(i j) lr((c)):i<j, c in K}$. Consider an
  arbitrary elementary matrix $bold(E)_(i j) lr((c))$, for $i!=j$. A moment's
  thought shows that there exist permutation matrices $bold(P),bold(Q) in N$
  such that $bold(P) bold(E)_(i j) lr((c)) bold(Q) in B$. For example, the
  operations
  $ mat(1, 0; c, 1) arrow.r mat(c, 1; 1, 0) arrow.r mat(1, c; 0, 1) $
  can be achieved by interchanging rows and columns, and hence by multiplication
  by elements of $N$. It follows from these remarks that
  $G=chevron.l B,N chevron.r$, and
  @def:finite-group-bn-pair@case:bn-pair-generation is proved.

  We have already noted that $B inter N=T$. Let $chevron.l v,w,... chevron.r$
  denote the subspace of $K^(n+1)$ generated by vectors $v,w,...$. Each matrix
  $n in N$ permutes the lines (= one-dimensional subspaces)
  $chevron.l e_1 chevron.r,...,
  chevron.l e_(n+1) chevron.r$. It follows that there exists a homomorphism from
  $N$ to the symmetric group $S_(n+1)$ whose kernel is the diagonal subgroup
  $T$. Thus @def:finite-group-bn-pair@case:bn-pair-normal-intersection is
  proved, and we have $N/(B inter N) approx S_(n+1)$, where $N/(B inter N)$ will
  turn out to be the Weyl group, once we have verified the other axioms.

  By Exercise~@exc:type-a-root-system, the transpositions
  ${tau_i=(i,i+1),1<=i<=n}$ generate $S_(n+1)$. For each $i$, let
  $dot(s)_i in N$ be the element that interchanges the basis vectors
  ${e_i,e_(i+1)}$ and leaves the others fixed. The homomorphism
  $N arrow.r S_(n+1)$ maps $dot(s)_i$ onto $tau_i$, for $1<=i<=n$; hence
  $W=N/(B inter N)$ is generated by the images $s_i in W$ of the elements
  $dot(s)_i$, $1<=i<=n$, and so
  $
    N/(B inter N)=chevron.l s_1,...,s_n chevron.r,
    quad s_i^2=1, quad 1<=i<=n.
  $
  We clearly have
  $ dot(s)_i B dot(s)_i!=B, quad 1<=i<=n, $
  and @eq:bn-pair-nondegeneracy-axiom holds.

  It remains to prove @eq:bn-pair-double-coset-axiom, namely that
  $ dot(s)_i B dot(w) subset.eq B dot(w) B union B dot(s)_i dot(w) B $
  for each $s_i$ and each $w in N/(B inter N)$ (where, as usual, $dot(w)$
  denotes a coset representative in $N$ of the element $w in N/(B inter N)$). It
  is clearly sufficient to prove
  $ dot(s)_i B subset.eq B B' union B dot(s)_i B' $
  <eq:linear-bn-pair-rank-two-reduction>
  where $B'=dot(w) B dot(w)^(-1)$.

  For each $i$, $1<=i<=n$, let $G_i$ be the subgroup of $G$ leaving invariant
  the two-dimensional subspace $chevron.l e_i,e_(i+1) chevron.r$ and fixing the
  other basis vectors. Then #source(598) $G_i approx GL_2 lr((K))$ and
  $dot(s)_i in G_i$. Moreover, we have
  $
    G_i B=B G_i,
  $ <eq:adjacent-linear-parabolic-product>
  which is easily verified since both sides are characterized as the set of all
  elements $g in G$ such that $g e_j in chevron.l e_1,...,e_j chevron.r$, if
  $j!=i$, and $g e_i in chevron.l e_1,...,e_(i+1) chevron.r$. By
  @eq:adjacent-linear-parabolic-product, $dot(s)_i B subset.eq B G_i$, and hence
  @eq:linear-bn-pair-rank-two-reduction will follow once we prove that
  $ G_i subset.eq B B' union B dot(s)_i B'. $
  For this, in turn, it suffices to show that
  $
    G_i subset.eq (B inter G_i)(B' inter G_i)
    union (B inter G_i) dot(s)_i (B' inter G_i).
  $
  If we identify $G_i$ with $GL_2 lr((K))$, then $B inter G_i$ is identified
  with the subgroup $B_2$ of upper triangular matrices in $GL_2 lr((K))$. Let
  $B_2^-$ denote the group of lower triangular matrices in $GL_2 lr((K))$.

  Now consider $B' inter G_i=dot(w) B dot(w)^(-1) inter G_i$. We assert that
  this group is identified with either $B_2$ or $B_2^-$; the first case occurs
  when $dot(w) B dot(w)^(-1) inter G_i$ carries $chevron.l e_i chevron.r$ onto
  itself, while the second case occurs when $dot(w) B dot(w)^(-1) inter G_i$
  carries $chevron.l e_(i+1) chevron.r$ to itself. To verify this assertion, we
  observe that the subgroup $dot(w)^(-1) G_i dot(w)$ stabilizes the subspace
  $dot(w)^(-1) chevron.l e_i,e_(i+1) chevron.r$ and fixes the lines
  $chevron.l dot(w)^(-1) e_k chevron.r$, $k!=i,i+1$. Then
  $dot(w)^(-1) chevron.l e_i chevron.r=chevron.l e_k chevron.r$,
  $dot(w)^(-1) chevron.l e_(i+1) chevron.r=chevron.l e_l chevron.r$ for some
  $k!=l$, $1<=k,l<=n+1$, and $B inter dot(w)^(-1) G_i dot(w)$ fixes either
  $chevron.l e_k chevron.r$ or $chevron.l e_l chevron.r$ by the definition of
  $B$. This means that $dot(w) B dot(w)^(-1) inter G_i$ stabilizes either
  $chevron.l e_i chevron.r$ or $chevron.l e_(i+1) chevron.r$, proving our
  assertion.

  In order to complete the proof, it is therefore sufficient to prove the two
  statements:
  $
    GL_2 lr((K))=B_2 union B_2 dot(s) B_2, quad "and"
    GL_2 lr((K))=B_2 B_2^- union B_2 dot(s) B_2^-,
  $
  where $dot(s)=mat(0, 1; 1, 0)$. The first is easily shown as follows. If
  $g in GL_2 lr((K))$ and $g in.not B_2$, then
  $ g=mat(a, b; c, d), quad "where" c!=0. $
  Then $bold(E)_12 lr((t)) in B_2$ for all $t in K$, and
  $ bold(E)_12 lr((-a c^(-1))) g=mat(0, ast; ast, ast). $
  It follows that
  $ s bold(E)_12 lr((-a c^(-1))) g in B_2, $
  and $g in B_2 s B_2$, as required.

  #source(599)
  For the second statement, we note that $s^2=1$ and $s B_2 s=B_2^-$. Using the
  first statement, we have
  $
    GL_2 lr((K))=GL_2 lr((K)) dot(s)
    =B_2 dot(s) union B_2 dot(s) B_2 dot(s)
    subset.eq B_2 dot(s) union B_2 B_2^-.
  $
  This proves the second statement, and completes the proof of the theorem.
]

_Remarks._ We leave it to the reader to check that the preceding argument,
followed step by step, also produces a $B N$-pair in the _unimodular group_
$ SL_(n+1) lr((K))={g in GL_(n+1) lr((K)):det g=1}, $
where now the subgroups $B,N$, and $T$ are the intersections with
$SL_(n+1) lr((K))$ of the corresponding groups for $GL_(n+1) lr((K))$. Since the
center $Z(SL_(n+1) lr((K)))$ consists of the scalar matrices
${xi dot I:xi in K,xi^(n+1)=1}$, we see that $Z(SL_(n+1) lr((K))) subset.eq T$.
It follows that the _projective unimodular group_
$ upright("PSL")_(n+1) lr((K))=frac(SL_(n+1) lr((K)), Z(SL_(n+1) lr((K)))) $
also has a $B N$-pair, in which the subgroups $B,N$, and $T$ are the images in
$upright("PSL")_(n+1) lr((K))$ of the corresponding groups in
$SL_(n+1) lr((K))$, under the natural map
$SL_(n+1) lr((K)) arrow.r upright("PSL")_(n+1) lr((K))$.

The other classical groups over finite fields—the orthogonal, symplectic, and
unitary groups—also have $B N$-pairs.#footnote[
  For an elementary construction of $B N$-pairs in the other classical groups,
  along the lines of the preceding discussion, see Bourbaki @bib:Bourbaki1968,
  Ex. 20, p. 53.
] <fn:classical-bn-pair-constructions>
These are examples of Chevalley groups. The Chevalley groups can all be
constructed by a uniform procedure. The general method used for their
construction also produces a $B N$-pair in each case (see Chevalley
@bib:Chevalley1955, Steinberg @bib:Steinberg1967, or Carter @bib:Carter1972b).

The finite Chevalley groups can also be obtained from the groups of
$bold(F)_q$-rational points on connected reductive affine algebraic groups
defined over finite fields $bold(F)_q$ (see Steinberg @bib:Steinberg1967,
@bib:Steinberg1968, Borel–Tits @bib:Borel1965).

=== Parabolic Subgroups of Finite Groups with $B N$-Pairs
<ss:bn-pair-parabolic-subgroups>

Throughout this subsection, $G$ denotes a finite group with a $B N$-pair. We let
$W$ denote the Weyl group of $G$, and $S$ the set of distinguished generators in
$W$. By @th:bn-pair-weyl-coxeter-system, the pair $(W,S)$ is a finite Coxeter
system, so we may apply the results of §§~@ss:coxeter-groups,
@ss:finite-coxeter-parabolic-subgroups to $(W,S)$. As in
§~@ss:bruhat-decomposition, we use the notation $dot(w)$ to denote a coset
representative in $N$ of an element $w in W$.

#theorem(title: [Tits @bib:Tits1962])[
  Let $J subset.eq S$, let $W_J$ be the parabolic subgroup of $W$ associated
  with $J$, as in @def:coxeter-parabolic-subgroup, and set $P_J=B W_J B$. Then
  $P_J$ is a subgroup of $G$ containing $B$, and moreover, every subgroup
  containing $B$ coincides with some $P_J$.
] <th:bn-pair-overgroups-borel>

#proof[
  #source(600)
  In order to prove that $P_J$ is a subgroup, it is sufficient to check that
  $g P_J=P_J$ for each $g in P_J$. This is clearly true if $g in B$, so it is
  enough to verify that $dot(w) P_J=P_J$ for each $w in W_J$. Since $W_J$ is
  generated by $J$, we only have to prove that $dot(s) P_J=P_J$ for $s in J$,
  and this is immediate by @eq:bn-pair-double-coset-axiom.

  To show that the ${P_J}$ are the only subgroups containing $B$, we first
  prove:
]

#lemma[
  Let $w=s_(i(1)) dots s_(i(m))$ be a reduced product of elements of $S$, and
  let $J={s_(i(1)),...,s_(i(m))}$. Then
  $
    chevron.l B,dot(w) chevron.r
    =chevron.l B,dot(w) B dot(w)^(-1) chevron.r=P_J.
  $
] <lem:bruhat-word-generated-parabolic>

#proof[
  We clearly have $chevron.l B,dot(w) B dot(w)^(-1) chevron.r
  subset.eq chevron.l B,dot(w) chevron.r subset.eq P_J$. Since
  $l(s_(i(1))w)<l(w)$, we obtain
  $ s_(i(1)) B subset.eq chevron.l B,dot(w) B dot(w)^(-1) chevron.r $
  by @cor:bn-pair-length-properties@case:bn-descent-conjugate-borel. Continuing,
  we have $l(s_(i(2))s_(i(1))w)<l(s_(i(1))w)$, so
  $
    s_(i(2)) B subset.eq
    chevron.l B,dot(s)_(i(1)) dot(w) B dot(w)^(-1)
    dot(s)_(i(1))^(-1) chevron.r
    subset.eq chevron.l B,dot(w) B dot(w)^(-1) chevron.r,
  $
  by the first step. We obtain finally
  $P_J subset.eq chevron.l B,dot(w) B dot(w)^(-1) chevron.r$, completing the
  proof of the lemma.
]

We can now finish the proof of the theorem. Any subgroup $Q$ containing $B$ is a
union of $(B,B)$-double cosets, so by @th:bruhat-decomposition we have
$ Q=union.big B dot(w) B, quad dot(w) in N inter Q. $
Then $Q=chevron.l B,dot(w):dot(w) in N inter Q chevron.r$. By
Lemma~@lem:bruhat-word-generated-parabolic, each subgroup
$chevron.l B,dot(w) chevron.r=P_J$ for some $J$ and each $P_J$ is contained in
$Q$ (for $dot(w) in N inter Q$). Letting
$ I=union.big_(P_J subset.eq Q) J, $
we have $P_I subset.eq Q$ and $Q subset.eq P_I$, completing the proof of
@th:bn-pair-overgroups-borel.

#definition[
  The subgroups of $G$ containing a fixed Borel subgroup $B$ of $G$ are called
  _standard parabolic subgroups_ of $G$; the set of all _parabolic subgroups_ of
  $G$ consists of a set of standard parabolic subgroups, and their
  $G$-conjugates. In particular, $B$ is the standard Borel subgroup.
] <def:bn-pair-parabolic-subgroups>
#idx("Parabolic subgroups", target: [@def:bn-pair-parabolic-subgroups])

By @th:bn-pair-overgroups-borel, the standard parabolic subgroups
$P supset.eq B$ are the subgroups ${P_I}_(I subset.eq S)$. Each standard
parabolic subgroup $P_I$ has a $B N$-pair with Borel subgroup $B$ and Weyl group
$W_I$.

We next have:

#proposition[
  Let $w in W$. Then $w$ belongs to the set of distinguished #source(601)
  generators $S$ of $W$ if and only if $w!=1$ and
  $ B union B w B $
  is a subgroup of $G$.
] <prop:bn-pair-distinguished-generators-intrinsic>

The proof is an easy application of Lemma~@lem:bruhat-word-generated-parabolic,
and is left to the reader. Note that this result proves the uniqueness of the
set of distinguished generators $S$. The next result will imply that $S$ is a
minimal set of generators (see also
@prop:standard-parabolic-properties@case:coxeter-generators-minimal).

#proposition[
  Let $I,J subset.eq S$. Then
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [#case[$I subset.eq J arrow.l.r P_I subset.eq P_J$.]
      <case:bn-parabolic-inclusion>],
    [#case[$P_I inter P_J=P_(I inter J)$.]
      <case:bn-parabolic-intersection>],
    [#case[
      The map $I arrow.r P_I$ gives a bijection from the subsets of $S$ onto the
      set of standard parabolic subgroups.
    ] <case:bn-parabolic-subset-bijection>],
  )
] <prop:bn-pair-parabolic-lattice>

#proof[
  @case:bn-parabolic-inclusion Suppose $P_I subset.eq P_J$, and let $s in I$.
  Then $B s B=B w B$ for some $w in W_J$, hence $s in W_J$ and $s in J$ by
  @prop:standard-parabolic-reduced-words. The reverse implication is clear, and
  part~@case:bn-parabolic-inclusion is proved.

  @case:bn-parabolic-intersection By @th:bn-pair-overgroups-borel,
  $P_I inter P_J=P_K$ for some $K subset.eq S$. Then $K subset.eq I inter J$ by
  part~@case:bn-parabolic-inclusion. On the other hand,
  $P_(I inter J) subset.eq P_K$, so $I inter J subset.eq K$, also by
  part~@case:bn-parabolic-inclusion.

  @case:bn-parabolic-subset-bijection The map $I arrow.r P_I$ is surjective, by
  @th:bn-pair-overgroups-borel. It is also injective, since $P_I=P_J$ implies
  $I=J$, by part~@case:bn-parabolic-inclusion, completing the proof.
]

#corollary[
  The set $S$ is a minimal set of generators of $W$.
] <cor:bn-pair-generators-minimal>

#theorem[
  Each parabolic subgroup $P$ is its own normalizer in $G$, that is,
  $N_G lr((P))=P$. More generally, if $P$ and $Q$ are parabolic subgroups such
  that $P inter Q$ is parabolic, then
  $ g P g^(-1) subset.eq Q arrow.l.r P subset.eq Q quad "and" g in Q. $
] <th:bn-pair-parabolic-normalizers-containment>

#proof[
  For the first statement, we may assume $P=P_I$ for some $I subset.eq S$. Then
  $N_G lr((P_I))$ contains $B$, so $N_G lr((P_I))=P_J$ for $J supset.eq I$, by
  @th:bn-pair-overgroups-borel and @prop:bn-pair-parabolic-lattice. Now let
  $s in J$; then $s B s^(-1) subset.eq P_I$, since $B subset.eq P_I$. Therefore
  $ chevron.l B,s B s^(-1) chevron.r subset.eq P_I, $
  so $s in I$, by @lem:bruhat-word-generated-parabolic and
  @prop:bn-pair-parabolic-lattice, proving the first statement.

  To prove the second part, suppose that $P inter Q$ is parabolic. We may then
  assume that $P=P_I$, $Q=P_J$ for some $I,J subset.eq S$, by definition and by
  @th:bn-pair-overgroups-borel. It is obvious that if $P_I subset.eq P_J$ and
  $g in P_J$, then $g P_I g^(-1) subset.eq P_J$. Conversely, let
  $g P_I g^(-1) subset.eq P_J$ where $I,J subset.eq S$, and $g in G$. By
  @th:bruhat-decomposition, $g in B w B$ for some $w in W$, and since
  $B subset.eq P_I inter P_J$, we have $w B w^(-1) subset.eq P_J$, hence
  $chevron.l B,w B w^(-1) chevron.r subset.eq P_J$, and
  $g in B w B subset.eq P_J$, by @lem:bruhat-word-generated-parabolic. We now
  have $g P_I g^(-1) subset.eq P_J$, and $g in P_J$, so $P_I subset.eq P_J$,
  completing the proof.
]

#source(602)
#corollary[
  Each parabolic subgroup of $G$ is conjugate to a unique standard parabolic
  subgroup $P_I$, for some $I subset.eq S$.
] <cor:bn-pair-parabolic-unique-standard-conjugate>

The last result in this section shows a further connection between parabolic
subgroups of $G$ and parabolic subgroups of $W$.

#theorem[
  Let $I,J subset.eq S$. There exists a bijection of double cosets
  $ W_I backslash W slash W_J arrow.l.r P_I backslash G slash P_J $
  given by
  $ W_I w W_J arrow.l.r B W_I w W_J B=P_I w P_J. $
] <th:bn-pair-parabolic-double-cosets>

#proof[
  Since $P_I=B W_I B$ and $P_J=B W_J B$, we have
  $ B W_I w W_J B subset.eq P_I w P_J. $
  <eq:bn-parabolic-double-coset-inclusion>
  We now prove the reverse inclusion. Let $w'$ range over all the elements of
  $W_I$, and $w''$ over $W_J$. Since
  $
    P_I=union.big_(w') B w' B quad "and"
    P_J=union.big_(w'') B w'' B,
  $
  we need only establish that
  $ B w' B w B w'' B subset.eq B W_I w W_J B. $
  This is immediate by repeated use of @eq:bn-pair-double-coset-axiom. We have
  thus established the equality of the two expressions in
  @eq:bn-parabolic-double-coset-inclusion. The existence of the bijection now
  follows from the uniqueness part of the Bruhat decomposition
  @th:bruhat-decomposition, completing the proof.
]
